{
    "title": "National Pearl Harbor Remembrance Day, 2025",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 10, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-10/pdf/2025-22536.pdf",
    "summary": "The President has proclaimed December 7, 2025, as National Pearl Harbor Remembrance Day, honoring the 2,403 Americans who died in the 1941 attack and calling for flags to be flown at half\u2011staff and for appropriate ceremonies across the country. The statement reflects on the nation\u2019s entry into World War II, praises the sacrifices of the \u201cGreatest Generation,\u201d underscores today\u2019s close U.S.\u2013Japan partnership, and urges continued vigilance.\n\nThis proclamation benefits veterans, survivors, and the families of the fallen by recognizing their loss and service in a visible, nationwide way. It also provides a clear moment for schools, communities, and organizations to teach history, reflect on resilience, and show support for military members past and present. Government agencies and civic groups get straightforward guidance on observance, making it easier to coordinate commemorative events. The reference to a strong U.S.\u2013Japan alliance highlights reconciliation and cooperation, which may resonate with those who value diplomacy.\n\nAt the same time, some people may find the day emotionally difficult, especially survivors and families who must relive painful memories. Others may be uncomfortable with the proclamation\u2019s strong language about military readiness, preferring a quieter tone focused solely on remembrance. A few may worry about the potential for symbolism to be politicized, or simply face the logistical burden of implementing half\u2011staff orders and ceremonies on short notice. There can also be differing views on how best to honor the past while speaking to present\u2011day security.\n\nOverall, the proclamation reaffirms an annual, congressionally recognized day of remembrance, balancing tribute to sacrifice with a message of preparedness and a reminder of how former adversaries became close partners.",
